  "id": "COMPOUND:688 - DISEASE:1010",
  "verbalization": "Methazolamide is implicated in the pathogenesis of Enterocolitis, Pseudomembranous, through a series of mechanistic pathways involving pseudogenes and regulatory proteins. The evidence suggests that Methazolamide affects the immunoglobulin kappa variable 2D-36 pseudogene, which in turn contributes to the development of the condition. This pseudogene is linked to Seborrhea NOS and regulator of G protein signaling 2, both of which are involved in the progression of Enterocolitis, Pseudomembranous. These findings indicate a potential role for Methazolamide in modulating the expression of these pseudogenes, thereby influencing the disease process. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible mechanism by which Methazolamide could contribute to the development of pseudomembranous enterocolitis, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence supports a mechanistic link, but further research is needed to confirm the direct effect of Methazolamide on these molecular targets.",
